WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.580
Of all the rabbit holes I get stuck in 
on the internet

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.025
I don’t know any quite as powerful 
as Google Earth.

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:15.269
Seeing beautiful patterns from above...

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:17.030
Dropping down into street view...

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.512
And seeing the planet in ways I would never
get to see in person.

00:00:22.892 --> 00:00:26.482
So when I came across this post on Reddit,
I was fascinated.

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:30.881
It described “undocumented markings” in
Algeria, in the middle of the Sahara

00:00:30.881 --> 00:00:33.719
near a location called “Tebalbalet tomb.”

00:00:33.719 --> 00:00:36.144
Visible on Google Earth.

00:00:36.144 --> 00:00:41.284
There were 22 of them, each with 12 
“surrounding things”, 42 meters in diameter

00:00:41.284 --> 00:00:45.642
420 meters apart, at longitude 4'20 East.

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:47.551
It almost sounded like a joke.

00:00:47.551 --> 00:00:50.376
But then I copied the coordinates and I looked.

00:00:52.893 --> 00:00:56.899
There they were: identical circles in an almost
perfect line.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:01.629
160 kilometers from any signs of life in the
world’s largest desert...

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:04.339
in the middle of the biggest country in Africa.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.375
This is a story about the limits of what you
can find out on the internet.

00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:13.735
About all the different ways of
looking at the same thing.

00:01:13.735 --> 00:01:16.488
And about going all the way there.

00:01:16.488 --> 00:01:20.529
Over the course of the last 20 weeks, 
we filmed every step of the process

00:01:20.529 --> 00:01:23.801
as we tried to figure out one thing...

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:25.847
What could these circles be?

00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:31.805
So this whole story starts back 
in September 2021

00:01:31.805 --> 00:01:33.975
when I first saw the Reddit post.

00:01:33.975 --> 00:01:36.078
I wanted to figure out what 
these “markings” were

00:01:36.078 --> 00:01:39.595
and make a video out of the entire 
reporting process.

00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:40.757
No matter how long it took.

00:01:40.757 --> 00:01:42.338
Because the answer had to be out there.

00:01:42.338 --> 00:01:45.814
And, step one, I knew I was going to have
to send some emails.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:48.566
For weeks, I reached out to everyone 
I could think of:

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:51.212
Algerian experts, officials, tour groups...

00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:54.164
even the closest hotel, in a city 
called Aïn Salah.

00:01:54.164 --> 00:01:57.915
I read up on the town the circles were located
closest to: Foggaret Ezzaouia.

00:01:57.915 --> 00:01:59.786
I asked the commenters on the Reddit post...

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:03.515
and we even tracked down a Twitter account
we thought was the same Will K

00:02:03.515 --> 00:02:07.916
who posted this question to several subreddits
before deleting his Reddit account.

00:02:07.916 --> 00:02:09.703
I tried English and French...

00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:11.146
organizations, academics, locals...

00:02:11.146 --> 00:02:12.495
And then...

00:02:13.327 --> 00:02:14.473
I waited.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.712
But there was one easy thing to clear up first.

00:02:19.712 --> 00:02:21.840
Were these circles real?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.081
Or were they just some kind of 
satellite imaging glitch?

00:02:25.081 --> 00:02:27.111
So I asked a teammate who 
works with maps a lot:

00:02:27.111 --> 00:02:29.309
Sam, he produces our series Atlas.

00:02:29.309 --> 00:02:32.262
And he pointed me to the company that takes
a lot of the satellite pictures

00:02:32.262 --> 00:02:35.270
for Google Earth: Maxar Technologies.

00:02:38.303 --> 00:02:41.471
I feel very confident that those are indeed
on the ground

00:02:41.471 --> 00:02:44.788
because we see them in multiple images 
over multiple years.

00:02:44.788 --> 00:02:49.636
So, I know it wasn't an artifact of the processing
that Google might have done with our imagery.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:54.469
And then a colleague of mine who has spent
a decent amount of time studying this area

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:58.710
said, “You know, this is a very rich area
for oil and gas.”

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:03.411
“This looks very similar to what we see
when they're doing oil exploration.”

00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:06.152
Oil radically changed the course
of Algeria’s history.

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:08.324
"Oil from the wastelands of the desert..."

00:03:08.324 --> 00:03:11.287
"And it's believed that the Sahara is immensely rich in it."

00:03:11.287 --> 00:03:16.057
When oil and gas were discovered there 
in 1956, companies flocked to the region

00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:20.000
against the backdrop of a brutal 
decolonization war with France.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.805
Today, Algeria is one of the world’s top
exporters of natural gas.

00:03:23.805 --> 00:03:26.726
What Steve is talking about here 
is seismic surveys

00:03:26.726 --> 00:03:31.755
where geophysicists analyze the Earth’s surface 
by sending shock waves into the ground.

00:03:31.755 --> 00:03:34.165
Depending on how those seismic waves 
bounce back

00:03:34.165 --> 00:03:38.137
researchers can tell what resources can be
extracted from underground.

00:03:38.137 --> 00:03:42.566
Steve thought that, maybe, seismic pulses
from a specialized vehicle

00:03:42.566 --> 00:03:44.988
could produce something like this.

00:03:46.272 --> 00:03:48.637
So, we had a hypothesis.

00:03:48.637 --> 00:03:50.247
But I wanted a second opinion.

00:03:50.247 --> 00:03:53.146
So I asked Bob Hardage at 
the University of Texas

00:03:53.146 --> 00:03:56.452
one of the world’s leading experts 
on seismic imaging.

00:03:56.452 --> 00:03:58.737
He responded by email:

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:01.791
”I can assure you with 100-percent 
confidence that

00:04:01.791 --> 00:04:04.183
the features in this imagery 
are not seismic arrays

00:04:04.183 --> 00:04:06.702
used in oil and gas exploration.”

00:04:06.702 --> 00:04:09.172
First, the shapes themselves weren’t right.

00:04:09.172 --> 00:04:11.621
“...there will be hundreds of thousands
of receivers positioned

00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:13.470
as either a single straight line

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.642
or as hundreds of parallel straight lines.”

00:04:15.642 --> 00:04:18.202
I looked up pictures from NASA 
of seismic surveying

00:04:18.202 --> 00:04:19.739
and you can see what he means.

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.329
Second: the fact that we could even see them
meant they probably weren’t a seismic survey.

00:04:24.329 --> 00:04:27.709
“... the objective is to leave the landscape
like you found it."

00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:30.119
"If a seismic crew created something like
these features

00:04:30.119 --> 00:04:33.818
a return visit would be made 
to restore the landscape.”

00:04:33.818 --> 00:04:36.780
“I have no idea what the circles 
in the satellite image are."

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.191
"Whatever they are, the people who 
created them

00:04:39.191 --> 00:04:41.328
wanted those features to be permanent.”

00:04:42.224 --> 00:04:45.330
“Closeout: I don’t think we need to chat.”

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.340
Thanks Bob.

00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:49.608
So I kept Googling.

00:04:49.608 --> 00:04:53.759
I found geotagged pictures from the nearest
municipality, Foggaret Ezzaouia

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.049
on a site called mapio.net.

00:04:56.049 --> 00:04:59.710
These old stone wells sorta looked like 
they could be arranged in a circle.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.068
But reverse image searches were a dead end.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.315
I didn’t know what to do next.

00:05:04.315 --> 00:05:08.308
So we looped in Vox video’s senior researcher,
Melissa, to help me out.

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:09.968
So, I was trying to find what this thing was.

00:05:09.968 --> 00:05:13.248
I don't know if you remember 
from his original post

00:05:13.248 --> 00:05:16.181
he calls it the Tebalbalet tomb.

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:17.191
Do you remember that?

00:05:17.191 --> 00:05:20.856
So I found this article.

00:05:20.856 --> 00:05:26.774
This is from like 1985 — I mean, 
not 1985: 1885.

00:05:26.774 --> 00:05:35.384
The “Well of Tebalbalet” is at the 
latitude 27°20 and longitude 4°38.

00:05:35.384 --> 00:05:39.178
And that's approximately where what 
we're looking at is.

00:05:39.178 --> 00:05:43.651
And it says there are two circular tumuli.

00:05:43.651 --> 00:05:45.960
I had to Google that, I don't know that word.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.408
-Tumuli.

00:05:47.408 --> 00:05:49.012
What's a tumulus.

00:05:49.012 --> 00:05:50.157
Tumulus.

00:05:50.157 --> 00:05:52.411
It's an ancient burial mound.

00:05:52.411 --> 00:05:56.553
Which seems... that sounds about right.

00:05:56.553 --> 00:06:02.344
“... encompassed by two concentric mounds
in the form of rings, all of great regularity."

00:06:02.344 --> 00:06:08.889
"The two rings are respectively 30 and 21
meters in diameter, from crest to crest.”

00:06:08.889 --> 00:06:13.640
So a document from 1885 said that, around
this same area, there were

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.529
1) a bunch of wells, and

00:06:15.529 --> 00:06:19.437
2) tombs with “rings of great regularity.”

00:06:19.437 --> 00:06:21.191
Now, the sketches weren’t an exact match.

00:06:21.191 --> 00:06:26.189
But they got us thinking: what if these things
were actually really old?

00:06:26.189 --> 00:06:30.390
So I sent the pictures to a Tunisian archaeologist
who had done research in this area.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:35.348
We spoke in French because of decades of French
occupation in the 19th and 20th centuries

00:06:35.348 --> 00:06:39.094
French is still used in many contexts 
in Tunisia and Algeria.

00:06:39.094 --> 00:06:41.205
And she had a new clue.

00:06:41.205 --> 00:06:43.586
[in French] These monuments, they are 
without a doubt

00:06:43.586 --> 00:06:45.870
[in French] because I know Aïn Salah very well...

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.953
[in French] These monuments are related to...

00:06:47.953 --> 00:06:48.906
[in French] Water.

00:06:50.369 --> 00:06:53.351
[in French] It's a desert environment, it's the 
Sahara.

00:06:53.351 --> 00:06:57.208
[in French] It is practically the hottest place 
in the Maghreb.

00:06:57.208 --> 00:07:00.245
[in French] It’s an area which is very well known

00:07:00.245 --> 00:07:04.016
for the difficulties of this heat there, 
and for the water harvest.

00:07:04.016 --> 00:07:05.919
[in French] So the people, they dig.

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:08.250
[in French] It has a name: the Foggaras.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Foggara.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.799
It’s the North African name for a 2,500-year-old
style of irrigation system

00:07:15.799 --> 00:07:19.039
that goes by many names, but is often called
a qanat.

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:21.757
Builders dig a well at an 
elevated point on a slope

00:07:21.757 --> 00:07:24.227
deep enough to tap into groundwater.

00:07:24.227 --> 00:07:26.919
They then dig parallel shafts 
at regular intervals.

00:07:26.919 --> 00:07:30.299
These provide air flow for diggers as they
create an underground channel

00:07:30.299 --> 00:07:32.490
all the way back to the main well.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.188
With a slope of 1 or 2 degrees, the channel
carries water long distances

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:38.549
powered by gravity alone.

00:07:38.549 --> 00:07:42.068
In a part of the world with barely any rain
and no running rivers

00:07:42.068 --> 00:07:46.408
this technology can provide water
for crops, livestock, and people

00:07:46.408 --> 00:07:47.609
year round...

00:07:47.609 --> 00:07:50.438
making human-made oases possible.

00:07:50.438 --> 00:07:51.538
[in French] It's curious, eh?

00:07:53.146 --> 00:07:55.228
This was the most promising lead yet.

00:07:55.228 --> 00:07:59.805
It explained the desert location, the circular
shape, the regularity, and spacing.

00:07:59.805 --> 00:08:04.037
Even the closest municipality’s name,
Foggaret Ezzaouia, is named after foggaras.

00:08:04.037 --> 00:08:07.200
And those mapio pictures of wells 
started to make sense.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.718
But I wanted to run it by more people 
who had studied qanats.

00:08:11.718 --> 00:08:15.078
Qanats are actually more than just 
water infrastructures.

00:08:15.078 --> 00:08:17.412
I think they are the very raison d'etre:

00:08:17.412 --> 00:08:20.670
the basis of habitation in such harsh climates.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:25.349
They start from outside of the city, 
but then they usually end up

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:27.885
into the city or into agricultural lands.

00:08:27.885 --> 00:08:29.609
But when it came to our circles...

00:08:29.609 --> 00:08:31.494
I have no take on it, honestly.

00:08:34.658 --> 00:08:36.640
I'm looking at it now.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.252
Right.

00:08:37.252 --> 00:08:38.640
Okay, that's interesting.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.603
There's something like 20 of them in a row.

00:08:40.603 --> 00:08:43.796
Yeah. So that's definitely a foggara.

00:08:43.796 --> 00:08:45.850
So at the end of that, there should be a town.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.320
There should be an oasis or something.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.733
But if there isn't, that means that probably
the water in the qanat

00:08:51.733 --> 00:08:53.347
or foggara has dried up since a long time.

00:08:53.347 --> 00:08:55.633
You should talk to Dale Lightfoot.

00:08:55.633 --> 00:08:57.850
He is the American geographer

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.670
who knows everything about qanats.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.379
These are what we're looking at.

00:09:02.379 --> 00:09:07.958
I couldn't even say with confidence whether
these are related to water collection.

00:09:07.958 --> 00:09:10.850
But I can tell you they're definitely not
qanats.

00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:13.884
We also found these pictures.

00:09:13.884 --> 00:09:17.080
Do you think these could be 
what the circles are?

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:23.737
What you're showing me pictures of here 
looks a lot like animal-drawn wells.

00:09:23.737 --> 00:09:25.654
I've seen these in a lot of places.

00:09:25.654 --> 00:09:27.962
To me, this is not the same thing.

00:09:27.962 --> 00:09:29.835
I think you're back to square one.

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:31.372
Back to square one, indeed.

00:09:31.372 --> 00:09:32.820
Don't rule out space aliens.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.415
I've heard they do crazy things, too.

00:09:37.505 --> 00:09:40.975
So they might be wells, but probably not a
qanat.

00:09:40.975 --> 00:09:43.825
And maybe not even related to water at all.

00:09:43.825 --> 00:09:45.413
Could we at least rule that out?

00:09:45.413 --> 00:09:48.765
That’s when Melissa found a database of
oases in the Sahara.

00:09:48.765 --> 00:09:51.905
With lists of the people who help manage 
their water supply.

00:09:51.905 --> 00:09:55.081
Like Mohammed Brik, a farmer in 
Laghouat, Algeria.

00:09:55.081 --> 00:09:59.524
I don't think it was done to fetch water.

00:09:59.524 --> 00:10:01.959
Because the point of going out 
to look for water

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:04.868
is to meet the needs of the population

00:10:04.868 --> 00:10:06.495
and agriculture.

00:10:06.495 --> 00:10:10.000
If there’s nothing for 160 kilometers

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.090
then that’s not a valid hypothesis.

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:16.303
Right. Because there is no village, no...

00:10:16.303 --> 00:10:17.347
There’s no village.

00:10:17.347 --> 00:10:18.265
There’s no garden.

00:10:18.265 --> 00:10:19.422
There's no oasis.

00:10:19.422 --> 00:10:20.803
There's nothing planted.

00:10:20.803 --> 00:10:22.990
There's no population.

00:10:24.065 --> 00:10:29.110
We were three months in and it seemed like
our most promising hypothesis yet 

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.477
was probably out.

00:10:31.477 --> 00:10:33.406
Then I got an email.

00:10:33.406 --> 00:10:35.745
Back in early October, Steve Wood 
promised to send me

00:10:35.745 --> 00:10:38.268
high-res images from Maxar’s archive.

00:10:38.268 --> 00:10:40.432
Finally, we had them.

00:10:43.596 --> 00:10:46.193
It was the clearest look we'd had yet.

00:10:46.193 --> 00:10:50.596
And Steve believed it showed a new detail:
tire tracks.

00:10:50.596 --> 00:10:55.046
If that was right, it would mean someone had
been there within the last century.

00:10:55.046 --> 00:10:56.409
I kept asking people.

00:10:56.409 --> 00:10:57.177
Historians...

00:10:57.177 --> 00:10:58.318
Algerian officials...

00:10:58.318 --> 00:10:59.109
Archaeologists...

00:10:59.109 --> 00:11:01.606
And nearby residents...

00:11:01.606 --> 00:11:04.226
But after a while, I felt stuck.

00:11:04.226 --> 00:11:06.937
Like we had exhausted what we could find out
on the internet.

00:11:06.937 --> 00:11:09.316
And there was nowhere else to go from here...

00:11:09.316 --> 00:11:11.846
except to the circles themselves.

00:11:13.159 --> 00:11:17.026
The longer this project went on, the more
I realized that we had a choice to make.

00:11:17.026 --> 00:11:20.887
We could keep interviewing more and more people,
get more and more theories

00:11:20.887 --> 00:11:24.136
and ultimately have no way to back them up.

00:11:24.136 --> 00:11:27.633
Or... we could figure out a way to get someone
there...

00:11:27.633 --> 00:11:28.878
Try to film it...

00:11:28.878 --> 00:11:32.600
and then, maybe, we could know for sure.

00:11:33.227 --> 00:11:37.116
So I asked my teammate Christina — who works
with journalists all over the world

00:11:37.116 --> 00:11:39.277
if she knew anyone in Algeria.

00:11:39.277 --> 00:11:42.945
And that led us to Samir Abchiche, a video
journalist in Algiers.

00:11:42.945 --> 00:11:44.751
I’m about to be a dad.

00:11:44.751 --> 00:11:47.336
So no more adventures for me after this.

00:11:47.336 --> 00:11:49.816
We hired Samir to be our on-the-ground journalist...

00:11:49.816 --> 00:11:53.366
to use his expertise in the area to help us
solve this mystery.

00:11:53.366 --> 00:11:55.434
The next part took months.

00:11:58.856 --> 00:12:00.866
We knew this wasn’t going to be 
an ordinary video shoot.

00:12:00.866 --> 00:12:03.815
We were asking him and his team 
to travel incredibly far

00:12:03.815 --> 00:12:06.197
to go do something potentially dangerous.

00:12:06.197 --> 00:12:08.326
But Samir took this story into his own hands.

00:12:08.326 --> 00:12:11.855
He was obsessing over every hypothesis, 
establishing local contacts

00:12:11.855 --> 00:12:15.195
figuring out all the details of how to get
a team of people from Algiers

00:12:15.195 --> 00:12:17.756
15 hours away by car to Aïn Salah

00:12:17.756 --> 00:12:20.226
and then deep into the desert 
where no roads go.

00:12:20.226 --> 00:12:23.594
Finally, Samir figured out how 
to make it happen.

00:12:23.594 --> 00:12:28.350
And at 7 pm on a cool February night, he and
his second cameraman Abdelate...

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:29.431
set off.

00:12:29.431 --> 00:12:31.873
And it begins.

00:12:31.873 --> 00:12:32.634
Shit.

00:12:32.634 --> 00:12:33.919
It does not begin.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.560
We can't find a hotel.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:46.595
They're all closed.

00:12:46.595 --> 00:12:48.993
And we're going to try Hotel El Djanoub.

00:12:48.993 --> 00:12:51.589
We have the Royal Suite.

00:12:54.992 --> 00:12:56.301
I just woke up.

00:13:01.137 --> 00:13:03.822
It's starting to get super hot.

00:13:06.419 --> 00:13:07.389
There's no service.

00:13:07.568 --> 00:13:09.015
It's yellow everywhere.

00:13:09.015 --> 00:13:09.687
But it’s beautiful.

00:13:09.687 --> 00:13:10.926
Yeah, but it’s beautiful.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:15.971
Which way to Ain Salah?

00:13:15.971 --> 00:13:17.606
300 km to Ain Salah.

00:13:17.606 --> 00:13:18.584
150 km.

00:13:18.584 --> 00:13:20.730
All we've seen is the horizon.

00:13:25.773 --> 00:13:28.586
They'd already spent 24 hours driving 
to get here.

00:13:28.586 --> 00:13:33.940
Now, they had to go another 160 km from 
Aïn Salah into the desert.

00:13:36.656 --> 00:13:38.689
But they had to pick someone else up first.

00:13:38.689 --> 00:13:41.800
Farid Ighilahriz, an archaeologist 
who used to lead Algeria’s

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.726
national archaeological research center

00:13:44.726 --> 00:13:48.153
and managed one of Algeria’s largest 
national parks.

00:13:48.153 --> 00:13:51.294
He’s here to help the team identify 
whatever they come across.

00:13:51.294 --> 00:13:52.473
How are we going to do this without cell service?

00:13:52.473 --> 00:13:54.617
No no, I made a map.

00:13:56.766 --> 00:13:58.692
From there, they prepared.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.230
They got groceries...

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:02.387
Bought fuel...

00:14:02.387 --> 00:14:04.287
Interviewed local officials...

00:14:04.287 --> 00:14:05.816
Planned the GPS route...

00:14:05.816 --> 00:14:07.282
And they assembled a team.

00:14:07.282 --> 00:14:09.282
A driver, an archeologist

00:14:09.282 --> 00:14:11.849
an assistant, and a desert guide. 

00:14:14.065 --> 00:14:17.009
It’s right about here that I lost communication
with Samir.

00:14:17.009 --> 00:14:20.160
And I wouldn’t be able to hear from him
until he was back in town...

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.835
With, hopefully, a definitive answer.

00:14:40.702 --> 00:14:43.224
No sandstorms, so that's good.

00:14:43.224 --> 00:14:44.791
That was making us nervous yesterday.

00:14:44.791 --> 00:14:46.824
It's still a bit risky,

00:14:46.824 --> 00:14:50.829
because nobody passes through this way.

00:14:50.829 --> 00:14:51.961
Almost nobody.

00:14:51.961 --> 00:14:53.638
And we're just two SUVs.

00:14:53.638 --> 00:14:55.534
This one is reliable,

00:14:55.534 --> 00:14:57.957
the other one, we don't really know.

00:15:12.881 --> 00:15:15.869
What’s weird is that as the crew got closer
and closer...

00:15:15.869 --> 00:15:19.612
They started finding signs pointing to every
one of our theories.

00:15:21.433 --> 00:15:25.186
First, tire marks from seismic survey trucks…

00:15:25.186 --> 00:15:26.666
Then, a well system...

00:15:26.666 --> 00:15:28.920
Water is always just three meters below.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.092
And finally, ancient tombs.

00:15:31.092 --> 00:15:32.601
We just saw something from far away.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.243
Yep, it's a tomb. 

00:15:36.657 --> 00:15:38.246
There’s another one.

00:15:38.813 --> 00:15:40.749
So this is a tumulus.

00:15:40.749 --> 00:15:44.826
It's one of the oldest kinds of funeral monuments.

00:16:05.498 --> 00:16:08.567
And on the morning of day two, they checked
the map, and…

00:16:08.567 --> 00:16:12.386
We're going roughly in the right direction.

00:16:12.386 --> 00:16:14.080
So we're 11 km from the first ones.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:16.600
I think we found them...

00:16:20.915 --> 00:16:24.795
False alarm...

00:16:24.795 --> 00:16:29.675
Did you see? We are approximately
500 meters from that place.

00:16:29.675 --> 00:16:31.843
Let's go!

00:16:31.843 --> 00:16:35.103
We got really excited, but they weren't there.

00:16:35.103 --> 00:16:38.373
Apparently they're just 500 meters away.

00:16:42.794 --> 00:16:46.032
We're not far.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:03.906
Right there, you can't see anything.

00:17:04.144 --> 00:17:05.226
You can't see anything.

00:17:10.868 --> 00:17:11.967
Right there, yeah?

00:17:15.699 --> 00:17:20.928
10 ... 11 ... 12 .... There we go, we have
all 12.

00:17:28.898 --> 00:17:32.728
After 160 kilometers of driving off-road in
the desert...

00:17:32.728 --> 00:17:33.728
they were there.

00:17:33.728 --> 00:17:36.599
The 22 circles, all in configuration.

00:17:38.928 --> 00:17:40.528
They were surprisingly faint.

00:17:40.528 --> 00:17:43.998
You might not notice them if you happened
to be passing by.

00:17:43.998 --> 00:17:47.360
As Samir and the team explored the area, they
found the next set.

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:48.780
And the next ones.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:52.050
This one's a bit clearer.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:55.386
The hole comes out of the ground.

00:17:55.386 --> 00:17:58.054
And a lot of them had something in common...

00:17:58.054 --> 00:17:59.641
metal wires.

00:17:59.641 --> 00:18:00.661
They’re connected.

00:18:00.661 --> 00:18:01.698
And there ... 

00:18:01.698 --> 00:18:05.490
Come see up close...

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:08.035
...they run underground.

00:18:08.035 --> 00:18:09.290
Can I dig a little?

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:10.142
No, no no.

00:18:10.142 --> 00:18:11.163
Not here?

00:18:11.163 --> 00:18:12.163
No.

00:18:12.163 --> 00:18:13.163
Sorry.

00:18:14.477 --> 00:18:16.217
So maybe they dug just a little bit...

00:18:16.217 --> 00:18:17.447
It's dynamite.

00:18:17.447 --> 00:18:18.829
Okay.

00:18:20.172 --> 00:18:23.088
Under these little mounds was dynamite.

00:18:24.133 --> 00:18:26.438
But here we have something else, too.

00:18:26.438 --> 00:18:29.641
We call these "attachments."

00:18:29.641 --> 00:18:32.704
It's what you'd put around a wooden crate.

00:18:32.704 --> 00:18:35.131
That's how they must have brought in the dynamite.

00:18:35.131 --> 00:18:38.101
That inscription reads SOTEMU — that’s
a French acronym for

00:18:38.101 --> 00:18:41.527
the “Tunisian company of explosives 
and ammunition.”

00:18:43.348 --> 00:18:45.705
But one of the wires looked different 
than the others.

00:18:45.705 --> 00:18:48.494
It still had a yellow plastic coating.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.458
This is where it got a little scary.

00:18:51.458 --> 00:18:53.403
Is it possible...

00:18:53.403 --> 00:18:56.162
Was this one not detonated yet?

00:19:01.893 --> 00:19:03.775
Well get out of there, don't stand there.

00:19:03.775 --> 00:19:05.552
Be careful.

00:19:05.552 --> 00:19:08.302
We gotta tell everyone to be careful.

00:19:08.302 --> 00:19:11.582
Eventually they decided that the dynamite
— if there was any left

00:19:11.582 --> 00:19:15.248
was probably harmless, because it would have
needed a detonator to go off.

00:19:16.113 --> 00:19:18.703
So they started to dig.

00:19:22.524 --> 00:19:24.723
It must go down at least a meter.

00:19:24.723 --> 00:19:26.622
But it was buried quite deep.

00:19:26.622 --> 00:19:30.102
So, at some point, to be safe, they stopped.

00:19:30.878 --> 00:19:34.414
And then they found a clue no one 
could have expected.

00:19:37.249 --> 00:19:39.119
Farid?

00:19:46.393 --> 00:19:47.715
Ah, yes.

00:19:49.565 --> 00:19:51.436
Bricato...

00:19:51.436 --> 00:19:52.262
Français...

00:19:52.262 --> 00:19:53.931
"Made in France."

00:19:53.931 --> 00:19:56.896
Old cans of sardines and tuna.

00:19:56.896 --> 00:19:58.922
Here we found a little tin can.

00:19:58.922 --> 00:20:00.000
That was used for food...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.490
...by those who worked here...

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.894
...who carried out this exploration.

00:20:04.894 --> 00:20:06.700
Oh, there’s color.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:12.070
Whoa...

00:20:14.607 --> 00:20:16.989
This could be the solution.

00:20:20.243 --> 00:20:23.948
So we knew what it was: dynamite, 
buried underground.

00:20:23.948 --> 00:20:26.114
And when Samir and the crew finally got home...

00:20:26.114 --> 00:20:28.251
I called him to hear all the details.

00:20:29.415 --> 00:20:30.794
My English, is it work for this?

00:20:30.794 --> 00:20:32.175
Yeah, it's perfect!

00:20:32.473 --> 00:20:36.880
We think that we have--we know the solution.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:41.376
So it's a method of searching for petroleum.

00:20:41.376 --> 00:20:44.085
But it was an old technique.

00:20:45.892 --> 00:20:48.803
At the very beginning of this journey, 
that’s one of the first things

00:20:48.803 --> 00:20:51.191
that anyone ever suggested.

00:20:51.191 --> 00:20:54.473
Is that it had to do with searching for petroleum.

00:20:54.473 --> 00:20:56.643
Which is crazy that it’s finally confirmed.

00:20:56.643 --> 00:21:01.120
It's the same thing that they do today 
just with dynamite

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.778
instead of more finely tuned technology.

00:21:04.555 --> 00:21:09.340
This is crazy, this is so much more wild
than I expected.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:12.379
Ironically, it put us right back right 
where we started.

00:21:13.513 --> 00:21:14.952
Seismic surveying.

00:21:14.952 --> 00:21:19.041
The circles are the remnants of surveyors
looking for resources underground.

00:21:19.041 --> 00:21:22.372
This whole time, that first guess was right.

00:21:22.372 --> 00:21:23.622
But only sort of.

00:21:23.622 --> 00:21:27.281
Because Bob Hardage at the University of Texas
was right when he said in that email

00:21:27.281 --> 00:21:29.752
that this doesn’t remotely resemble seismic
surveying…

00:21:29.752 --> 00:21:32.820
Because this isn’t how seismic surveying
works today.

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:35.732
It’s an older technique, from the early
days of surveying

00:21:35.732 --> 00:21:39.912
that uses dynamite explosions instead 
of vibration machines.

00:21:39.912 --> 00:21:43.873
The explosions would provide the seismic waves
that would reflect and refract off of the

00:21:43.873 --> 00:21:45.151
ground underneath

00:21:45.151 --> 00:21:50.021
and that would tell surveyors that something
— potentially something valuable, like oil

00:21:50.021 --> 00:21:53.671
was underground and worth digging for.

00:21:53.671 --> 00:21:57.314
The circles looked like this because of the
force of those dynamite explosions 

00:21:57.314 --> 00:21:58.930
happening underground.

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:01.818
From this moment, a new question came. 

00:22:01.818 --> 00:22:04.878
Who did this and when?

00:22:04.878 --> 00:22:07.506
Knowing it was a seismic survey wasn't enough.

00:22:07.506 --> 00:22:10.436
But we had one other clue from 
the desert to turn to...

00:22:10.436 --> 00:22:12.935
the sardine cans.

00:22:13.323 --> 00:22:14.904
I reached out to Saupiquet

00:22:14.904 --> 00:22:18.014
which seems to be the only one of these companies
that still exists

00:22:18.014 --> 00:22:20.816
but they said they couldn’t identify 
their age by photos.

00:22:20.816 --> 00:22:22.846
So I found someone who’s been collecting
sardine cans

00:22:22.846 --> 00:22:26.024
for over 40 years: Philippe Anginot.

00:22:26.024 --> 00:22:27.973
He even made a museum out of it.

00:22:27.973 --> 00:22:29.245
And I showed him the pictures.

00:22:29.245 --> 00:22:34.094
What we have here is what's called a three-body
can.

00:22:34.094 --> 00:22:36.187
So these are typical cans from the 1960s.

00:22:36.187 --> 00:22:39.019
After 1960

00:22:39.019 --> 00:22:41.856
the “Arsène Saupiquet Cannery”

00:22:41.856 --> 00:22:44.125
became the “Saupiquet Company.”

00:22:44.125 --> 00:22:49.236
When it's still labeled "Arsène," it's from
before 1960.

00:22:49.236 --> 00:22:53.755
So because this can was labeled Arsène Saupiquet,
we know that it was manufactured before they

00:22:53.755 --> 00:22:56.122
changed their name in 1960.

00:22:56.122 --> 00:23:00.413
Because of its 60s-style “three-body”
design, we know it’s probably from the very

00:23:00.413 --> 00:23:01.752
late 1950s.

00:23:01.752 --> 00:23:05.275
Granted, this is canned food, so it’s possible
that it was purchased years before it was

00:23:05.275 --> 00:23:06.475
actually eaten.

00:23:06.475 --> 00:23:08.771
But I think we can safely guess that these
cans were left behind 

00:23:08.771 --> 00:23:13.736
by an oil exploration crew sometime 
in the late 1950s.

00:23:13.736 --> 00:23:18.507
All that was left was to figure out who those
people were.

00:23:18.507 --> 00:23:22.017
Before going into the desert, Samir recorded
interviews with the experts that they met

00:23:22.017 --> 00:23:23.405
along the way.

00:23:23.405 --> 00:23:26.396
And there was one interview with someone who
actually would have been there

00:23:26.396 --> 00:23:27.396
in the late 1950s.

00:23:27.396 --> 00:23:31.450
The father of the desert guides,
who used to work as a guide himself.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:32.038
Peace be upon you.

00:23:32.724 --> 00:23:38.340
Here are the photos, Belhadj.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:41.090
I see the small holes placed like the hands
of a watch.

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.088
When did the drilling of [that area] take
place?

00:23:43.088 --> 00:23:52.003
In 1953, the vehicles came to Djebel
Beida to go to the probe.

00:23:52.003 --> 00:23:54.302
So this place existed and a
company was working there.

00:23:54.302 --> 00:23:56.273
Yes, it is true.

00:23:56.273 --> 00:23:58.653
What were they doing.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.610
I know that they were digging, that's all.

00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:04.075
What was their name?

00:24:04.075 --> 00:24:05.915
I no longer remember.

00:24:05.915 --> 00:24:07.415
But I believe CREPS.

00:24:07.415 --> 00:24:12.540
I know that, at that time, CREPS was
working.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:17.804
CREPS — a French acronym for the Sahara
Petroleum Research and Exploitation Company

00:24:17.804 --> 00:24:21.594
was a joint venture between 
the French government and Shell.

00:24:21.594 --> 00:24:26.604
CREPS had a permit to explore and extract
oil in this entire expanse of the Sahara

00:24:26.604 --> 00:24:29.583
from 1953 to 1958.

00:24:29.583 --> 00:24:34.321
Lining up that map with Google Maps shows
that the circles are inside that CREPS sector.

00:24:34.321 --> 00:24:38.596
And according to these French Senate records
they started geological surveys right away.

00:24:38.596 --> 00:24:42.755
Within that time, CREPS became the first company
to strike oil in the Sahara,

00:24:42.755 --> 00:24:45.076
in Edjeleh in 1956.

00:24:45.076 --> 00:24:48.133
This spurred a rush of oil companies into
the region.

00:24:48.133 --> 00:24:52.653
And the struggle over control of Saharan oil
became a centerpiece of France’s brutal

00:24:52.653 --> 00:24:54.817
war against Algerian Independence.

00:24:54.817 --> 00:24:57.205
"It was the end of nearly 
8 years of bloodshed."

00:24:57.205 --> 00:25:01.265
"And the African nation won its freedom
after 132 years."

00:25:01.265 --> 00:25:04.075
Even when Algeria won its independence in
1962

00:25:04.075 --> 00:25:07.704
France maintained rights to Saharan oil for
years to come.

00:25:08.152 --> 00:25:10.874
These circles are the scars of colonialism.

00:25:10.874 --> 00:25:14.794
They're evidence of one country’s attempts
to take the resources of another.

00:25:15.564 --> 00:25:19.637
And they’re only as isolated as they are
because oil wasn’t found there.

00:25:19.637 --> 00:25:23.407
Everywhere that it was, was transformed forever.

00:25:26.745 --> 00:25:29.796
So, we figured it out.

00:25:29.796 --> 00:25:34.896
These circles in the Sahara were made by French
CREPS employees looking for oil.

00:25:34.896 --> 00:25:37.337
They were made by underground dynamite explosions

00:25:37.337 --> 00:25:40.786
arranged in circles along a straight line
through the desert.

00:25:40.786 --> 00:25:45.316
And based on the dates of the CREPS permit,
and the types of cans they left behind

00:25:45.316 --> 00:25:50.455
we can safely say they were there around 1957
or 1958.

00:25:51.768 --> 00:25:54.067
When we figured it all out, I emailed Bob.

00:25:54.067 --> 00:25:56.067
And he said this:

00:25:56.067 --> 00:25:59.433
"You have certainly done a persistent
and thorough investigation."

00:25:59.433 --> 00:26:04.680
"I am comfortable with the conclusion that your features
are remnants of decades-old, first generation

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:07.277
analog recording of seismic data."

00:26:07.277 --> 00:26:09.516
"An unbelievable  preservation."

00:26:09.516 --> 00:26:13.240
"Comparing 1950s seismic equipment and 
today's seismic equipment

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:17.041
is similar to comparing propeller airplanes
and deep space rockets."

00:26:17.041 --> 00:26:19.570
"Essentially, there is no comparison

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:22.070
but two different worlds."

00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:23.525
"Well done."

00:26:23.525 --> 00:26:24.939
Thanks, Bob.

00:26:28.832 --> 00:26:31.910
We only know this thanks to the help of dozens
of people

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:35.635
someone’s sixty-five-year-old trash, a lot
of time on the internet

00:26:35.635 --> 00:26:38.405
and a long, brave journey into the desert.

00:26:38.405 --> 00:26:42.889
Of course, a story like this could always
keep going, more and more specific.

00:26:42.889 --> 00:26:44.239
But at some point

00:26:44.239 --> 00:26:48.679
to finish a story, we have to ask ourselves 
if the answer we have is satisfactory. 

00:26:50.133 --> 00:26:51.372
And I think this one is.

